 The function CONF_get1_default_config_file() determines the default
 configuration file pathname as follows.
 If the B<OPENSSL_CONF> environment variable is set its value is returned.
-Else the function returns the path obtained using
-L<X509_get_default_cert_area(3)> with the filename C<"openssl.cnf"> appended.
+On Unix, the function otherwise returns the first C<openssl.cnf> path that
+exists in C<OPENSSLDIR>, F</run/ssl>, or F</usr/lib/ssl>, in that order. An
+error other than a missing path selects that path so an inaccessible
+higher-priority file cannot expose lower configuration. If no file exists,
+the function returns F</usr/lib/ssl/openssl.cnf>. On other systems, it returns
+the path obtained using L<X509_get_default_cert_area(3)> with the filename
+C<"openssl.cnf"> appended.
 The caller is responsible for freeing any string returned.
 
